Chapter: DeskCboxDriftTest
  cites Works chapter GopDraw
  cites Works chapter GopDesk
  cites Works chapter GopScene
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 WORKS-60. A scene pane sizes its pixel and depth buffers ONCE, from the
 content box at the moment it opens (`gsc-new`, called with `dk-cbox-w` and
 `dk-cbox-h`). Every frame afterwards `gsc-place` sets the render target's
 `r3t-w` and `r3t-h` from the WINDOW rectangle, and a maximised window is the
 content box exactly. Nothing ties the two together, and `r3d-target-clear`
 fills `r3t-w * r3t-h` words into a buffer holding `bw * bh`.

 The content box is not a constant: it is the screen less a strip on each of
 the four edges, and which edges carry a strip is a setting the user changes
 while apps are open. So the question this asks is whether the box can GROW
 after a pane has sized itself, and by how many words, because that number is
 the overrun.

 The four edges are measured against each other rather than against a
 remembered constant: a change to `dk-task-h`, `dk-task-w` or the scale moves
 every row here together and the deltas are what carry the finding.

Section: Body

  dcd-yn : Boolean -> Text
  dcd-yn (v) = if v then "yes" else "NO"

 An empty registry with a real count cell, because `dk-strip-any` walks the
 focus ids through `dk-pill-live` and that reads the registry. Nothing is
 pinned and nothing is registered, so only the TASK edge contributes a strip
 and the four rows differ by exactly the thing under test.

  dcd-ws : Integer
  dcd-ws =
    let ws = alloc-zeroed 64 64
    in let n = poke-32 ws 0 0
    in ws

  dcd-ds : Integer -> Integer
  dcd-ds (e) =
    let ds = alloc-zeroed dk-ds-bytes 64
    in let ap = poke-32 ds dk-adorn-cell dk-adorn-default
    in let pei = dk-pedge-init ds
    in let wr = poke-32 ds dk-wr-cell dcd-ws
    in let sp = poke-32 ds dk-task-edge-cell e
    in ds

  dcd-name : Integer -> Text
  dcd-name (e) =
    if e == dk-edge-bottom then "bottom"
    else if e == dk-edge-top then "top"
    else if e == dk-edge-left then "left"
    else "right"

  dcd-w : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dcd-w (e) (w) (h) = dk-cbox-w w (dcd-ds e)

  dcd-h : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dcd-h (e) (w) (h) = dk-cbox-h w h (dcd-ds e)

  dcd-edge : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Text
  dcd-edge (e) (w) (h) =
    "  " & dcd-name e & " " & integer-to-text (dcd-w e w h) & "x" & integer-to-text (dcd-h e w h)
    & " = " & integer-to-text (dcd-w e w h * dcd-h e w h) & " words"

  dcd-edges : Integer, Integer -> Text
  dcd-edges (w) (h) =
    "edges at " & integer-to-text w & "x" & integer-to-text h & ":"
    & dcd-edge dk-edge-bottom w h
    & dcd-edge dk-edge-top w h
    & dcd-edge dk-edge-left w h
    & dcd-edge dk-edge-right w h

 THE OVERRUN IS THE PAIR, not either box alone. `open` is the edge the pane
 was born under and sized its buffers from; `now` is the edge in force when
 the frame clears. A positive delta is words written past the end of both the
 pixel and the depth buffer, every frame, for as long as the pane is up.

  dcd-drift : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Text
  dcd-drift (open) (now) (w) (h) =
    let cap = dcd-w open w h * dcd-h open w h
    in let want = dcd-w now w h * dcd-h now w h
    in "opened at the " & dcd-name open & ", cleared at the " & dcd-name now
       & ": buffer holds " & integer-to-text cap
       & "  the clear writes " & integer-to-text want
       & "  overruns " & dcd-yn (want > cap)
       & "  by " & integer-to-text (want - cap) & " words"

 A DEGENERATE BOX IS THE OTHER WAY THIS BREAKS, and it is asked separately
 because it needs no drift at all: `dk-cbox-w` and `dk-cbox-h` subtract four
 strips with no floor, and `gsc-new` hands the result straight to
 `alloc-bytes` without looking at it. If every row here is positive the
 degenerate box is NOT reachable by the task edge alone, which is a narrower
 claim than "not reachable".

  dcd-min : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dcd-min (a) (b) = if a < b then a else b

  dcd-min4 : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dcd-min4 (w) (h) =
    let a = dcd-w dk-edge-bottom w h
    in let b = dcd-w dk-edge-top w h
    in let c = dcd-w dk-edge-left w h
    in let d = dcd-w dk-edge-right w h
    in let e = dcd-h dk-edge-bottom w h
    in let f = dcd-h dk-edge-top w h
    in let g = dcd-h dk-edge-left w h
    in let i = dcd-h dk-edge-right w h
    in dcd-min (dcd-min (dcd-min a b) (dcd-min c d)) (dcd-min (dcd-min e f) (dcd-min g i))

  dcd-degen : Integer, Integer -> Text
  dcd-degen (w) (h) =
    let m = dcd-min4 w h
    in "degenerate probe at " & integer-to-text w & "x" & integer-to-text h
       & ": smallest box dimension over the four edges " & integer-to-text m
       & "  positive " & dcd-yn (m > 0)

 THE CLAMP IS ASKED ON THE SAME PAIR THE OVERRUN ROW MEASURED, and both
 halves are in the row: a clamp that did nothing leaves `fits` NO, and a
 measurement that had found no drift leaves `unclamped would not` NO, so
 neither the fix nor the defect can pass on the other's evidence.

  dcd-clamp : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Text
  dcd-clamp (open) (now) (w) (h) =
    let bw = dcd-w open w h
    in let bh = dcd-h open w h
    in let rw = dcd-w now w h
    in let rh = dcd-h now w h
    in "clamp: rect " & integer-to-text rw & "x" & integer-to-text rh
       & " into buffers " & integer-to-text bw & "x" & integer-to-text bh
       & " gives " & integer-to-text (gsc-fit rw bw) & "x" & integer-to-text (gsc-fit rh bh)
       & "  fits " & dcd-yn (gsc-fit rw bw * gsc-fit rh bh <= bw * bh)
       & "  unclamped would not " & dcd-yn (rw * rh > bw * bh)
       & "  a negative rect floors at zero " & dcd-yn (gsc-fit (0 - 1) bw == 0)
       & "  a rect inside the buffer is untouched " & dcd-yn (gsc-fit (bw - 7) bw == bw - 7)

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing =
    act
      print-line-uni (dcd-edges 1600 900)
      print-line-uni (dcd-drift dk-edge-bottom dk-edge-left 1600 900)
      print-line-uni (dcd-drift dk-edge-bottom dk-edge-right 1600 900)
      print-line-uni (dcd-drift dk-edge-left dk-edge-bottom 1600 900)
      print-line-uni (dcd-clamp dk-edge-bottom dk-edge-left 1600 900)
      print-line-uni (dcd-degen 1600 900)
      print-line-uni (dcd-degen 640 480)
      print-line-uni "desk-cbox-drift: reached the end"
    end
